  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nStorms yesterday evening and into the early overnight packed a \nrelative punch within an environment ripe for evaporatively enhanced \ndowndrafts. A number of areas experienced severe wind gusts and \nassociated wind damage. Also with relatively weak deep layer wind \nprofiles, outflows from storms resulted in chaotic storm motions. As \nof early this morning, a few mainly elevated showers and non-severe \nstorms remain, predominantly associated with weak mid-level \nshortwave activity and lingering outflow. These will generally \ndrift SE with the mid-level flow and gradually subside.\n\nThe stagnant / Omega Block pattern of late begins to shift and break \ndown today. This in response to a Polar Stream trough shunting the \nNew England cutoff low eastward and as that occurs, the western \nportion of the Omega Block, the PNW cutoff low, will begin lifting \nNE. More regionally, this too will push the mid-level ridge axis \nback eastward out of the High Plains and toward the Missouri and Mid \nto Upper Mississippi river Valleys. Unfortunately, that also means \nwell above normal temperatures continue for a bit longer, re-\nsurging/peaking on Tuesday. For today though, existing surface high \nover the Great Lakes will keep winds out of the E/SE and should \nyield temperatures a couple/few degrees cooler than seen Sunday. As \nmentioned just previously, Tuesday sees resurgence of heat though as \nwinds become more southerly as the surface high shifts eastward and \nthe 850mb thermal ridge angles overhead. Tuesday likely sees highs \nback to the mid 90s to 100 across the area. Given the current \nheadline orientation, Excessive Heat Warning for the KC Metro area \ncounties and areas W and S through Tuesday, an additional short-\nduration headline may be necessary for surrounding areas to the \nN/NE/E.\n\nBy late Tuesday, large scale pattern will take on a more zonal look \nacross most of CONUS. The previous PNW low will be situated over the \nCanadian Plains along with its attendant surface low. More \nimportantly, the trailing cold front will be dropping southward \nthrough the Dakotas and into Nebraska and Iowa by this time. This \nallows more substantial relief to the area Wednesday as the cold \nfront continues to drop southward. Expect highs to fall back into \nthe mid 80s across northern Missouri. Some uncertainty lingers in \nhow quickly the front will drop south Wednesday, leading to high \ntemperature spreads in excess of 7 deg F south of Highway 36. This \ntoo keeps the notion of a possible heat headline in play Wednesday \nfor southern areas where these wide spreads include the potential \nfor upper 90s to 100 and heat index values that would at least \napproach Advisory criteria. Shower and storm activity is also \nexpected as the front drops through. The threat for severe storms \nappears fairly muted with NBM SBCAPE probabilities of >1000 J/kg \nwidely 60 percent or less and supportive deep layer shear primarily \npost-frontal. Some noted dry air in the lowest levels may yield some \nwind potential though. The current SPC Day 2 and 3 Marginal Risks \nover portions of the area feel appropriate. Relief overspreads the \nentirety of the area by Thursday, with forecast highs ranging from \nthe low to mid 80s. \n\nLarge scale pattern looks to remain more zonal in nature through the \nweekend and beyond, which will also allow additional periodic \nchances for showers and storms. At this point, those chances appear \nto be in the Friday/Saturday and Monday/Tuesday time frames as \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance depict shortwave troughs sliding \nthrough.",
